# ── Frappe / ERPNext lead capture ────────────────────────────────
# Copy to .env for local work. In production set these in the Vercel
# project (Settings → Environment Variables).
#
# IMPORTANT: VITE_* values are baked in at BUILD time, not read at
# runtime. Setting them in Vercel does nothing until you redeploy.
#
# Leave VITE_FRAPPE_URL empty and the contact form degrades to a
# mailto: handoff, telling the visitor to send from their mail app
# rather than pretending the message was delivered.

# Base URL of the Frappe instance, no trailing slash.
# Example: VITE_FRAPPE_URL=https://erp.techbird.in
VITE_FRAPPE_URL=

# Route of a guest-submittable Web Form, used when the REST API refuses
# an anonymous write (401/403). Only needed if you go the Web Form route
# instead of granting Guest create permission on Lead.
# Defaults to "website-lead".
VITE_FRAPPE_WEB_FORM=

# ── Go-live checklist ────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. Set VITE_FRAPPE_URL above (or in Vercel) and redeploy.
# 2. Allow this site's origin in Frappe CORS (site_config.json →
#    allow_cors, or the reverse proxy). The form sends no credentials,
#    so a plain origin allowance is enough.
# 3. Permit anonymous lead creation, either by granting the Guest role
#    create permission on Lead, or by publishing a Web Form with
#    "Allow Guest to Submit" whose route matches VITE_FRAPPE_WEB_FORM.
# 4. Check buildLeadPayload() in src/lib/frappe.js against the Lead
#    doctype on your instance. Frappe rejects the whole submission if a
#    fieldname does not exist - `website_service_interest` is a custom
#    field and is the likely culprit if every lead starts failing.
# 5. Submit a real test lead and confirm it lands in Frappe. A failure
#    shows an error with the fallback email; details go to the console.
